.TH TAC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
tac \- print files with their lines in reverse order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tac
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tac
writes each
.I file
to standard output with its lines in reverse order, last line first.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
This
.B tac
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Files are written in the order given, and the lines of each file are
reversed on their own.
.B tac a b
writes the reversed
.I a
followed by the reversed
.IR b ,
unlike
.BR "cat a b | tac" ,
which writes the lines of
.I b
first.
.PP
A line ends at an LF.
A CR or NUL is an ordinary byte, so a CR LF line ending stays at the end
of its line.
An empty file writes nothing.
.PP
The last line of a file may have no LF.
It is written first, and without one, so it runs into whatever is
written next: the line before it or, if it is the only line of its
file, the last line of the next file.
.PP
.B tac
reads all of its input before it writes anything, and holds it in
memory with some overhead for each line.
Input that never ends, such as a program that runs forever, produces no
output.
.PP
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped.
A read error is reported too, and ends its file, but the lines read
before it are still written.
Either way, the remaining files are still written.
Operands are read from last to first, so the messages come in that
order, before any output.
.SH OPTIONS
.B tac
has no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
An argument that starts with a dash is an option wherever it appears,
even after the
.I file
operands, except
.BR \- ,
which is standard input.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a file whose name starts with a dash follows it or
is given with a leading
.BR ./ .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and elsewhere is an unknown
option.
.PP
Any other option prints an error and the usage message on standard error
and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that a later argument such as
.B \-x
is a file name.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B tac
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a
.I file
could not be opened or read (a directory is reported as
.BR "Is a directory" ),
memory ran out, or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show a file from its last line to its first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tac log.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the five most recent lines of a log, newest first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tac log.txt | head \-n 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the last line of a file that contains
.BR error :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tac log.txt | grep \-m1 error
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reverse the lines of two files joined together, not each on its own:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat a.txt b.txt | tac
.EE
.RE
.PP
A last line without an LF is written first, and runs into the line
before it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa\enb\(aq | tac
ba
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike GNU
.BR tac ,
this
.B tac
has no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
The GNU options
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-\-version
are errors, and a line always ends at an LF.
.PP
The length of a line is kept in an
.BR int ,
so a line of 2 GiB or more is not supported.
.PP
.B tac
does not close a file until it exits, and the C runtime allows about
500 open files.
Since operands are opened from last to first, with more operands than
that the first ones fail with
.BR "Too many open files" .
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, so CR LF line endings and Ctrl-Z
characters are copied unchanged.
.PP
File names may use drive letters, backslashes, and forward slashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names that busybox accepts, such as
.I /dev/null
and
.IR /dev/stdin ,
work as operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, non-ASCII characters typed at a
console arrive in the console's input code page, such as 437, not in
UTF-8.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
Ctrl-D is an ordinary character.
.PP
When standard output is a console, each line is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, unless the first of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
that is set is
.BR C .
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
A line that contains a NUL byte is written as is, with no conversion or
emulation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR rev (1)
